# Auto-ship + feature commits (FLOPS)

## Default: commit and push to live

FLOPS is early; Diego is the only user. After finishing an implementation (feature, fix, or rule he asked for):

- **Automatically** create feature-scoped commit(s) and push to `main` so Vercel + Render update the live web app.
- Do **not** ask “want me to push?” — assume yes.
- Only skip commit/push if Diego **explicitly** says not to (e.g. “don’t push”, “local only”, “hold deploy”).
- Do **not** invent a staging/dev gate or refuse the push “because it’s production.”
- Still never force-push, skip hooks, commit secrets (`.env`, `nutrition.db`), or update git config.

When real multi-user traffic arrives, revisit this rule and require a preview/dev check before `main`.

## Feature-scoped commits (rollback trail)

Every shippable change must land as **one clear feature commit** (or a short stack), not a mixed dump of unrelated WIP.

- **One concern per commit** — e.g. `feat(dashboard): collapse meal macros panel`, not “misc UI + auth + css”.
- Prefer Conventional Commits: `feat(scope): …`, `fix(scope): …`, `chore: …`, `docs: …`.
- Message focuses on **why / user-visible outcome** so `git log` is a feature timeline.
- If several features land in one session, **separate commits** then push once so Diego can `git revert <hash>` without undoing unrelated work.
- Stage only files for that feature; leave unrelated dirty files for their own commit.

## After push

Briefly note that live will update; hard-refresh if needed. If something breaks, revert that feature commit on `main` rather than rewriting history.
